        "content": "<p>This <a href=\"https://live.lean-lang.org/#codez=JYWwDg9gTgLgBAWQIYwBYBtgCMB0AZCAc2AGMcBRARwFdgA3HAEQFMAzAZwCgB6AWl87MoUaAC44ACgDWQgHbN0ASjhIohOAGIATHAis4AcgASSdlIBiwAB44QUg3BIRZMJMFnsVcWc7h0kmAAmuiQk1MLMsiTMuvpoMYEoKACeYMyeWMzu6oHMJOiqzIGcvNycGoTUqoEA%2BiDshJ7unO6B1CQw9DEmZpZWkubiACqpMdRwgEmEcCNpcNTKAO6oQsyccHAAPnB24uaSPRbWcObKUwd9x5wtsm0dXXAAChDoyebUUTDQcEsra5twAEliustgBhZzsGDDUZzSaPZ6vd4daB%2FLaMYDsABWEHcMAAytQQOIni83h8vlMSYjyVA4VSycioKi4KDVDB0sAkLIHiJAsSEQzPrTKQKkUK6aKaVdcvpck5ctDZnQJaSxRTpjCanBmQANAA0cBwQLgAF4AHxwHXMmoGnDgjzwADSpotjt1tvRWJxLgJIGOcAA4i7JHKILlLf7AKlEcFD4Z1gcU7sNrNgHK5PLD%2FqD5pDeTDMXjewA6zG83GEzx%2BIJhGJJDIoPIlCo1JodHpDOhWGBbPZHM5XO5PEhvL5%2FEEQmEIlEYu34jGkjBRhksrIcnkClAiiVuOtypVqnUGk1ZNdbp06DFO2ABnB%2BaqaYtlpvmTtS%2FKYhIr8dlHteBav%2BY0psIg4wSIMGqzOMUwzGMyiiCacAAN4AIKKmMAC%2BcJgXAyFwshpw4ZwMpwAAcqB4EwbC0EwvMt4IShaFzJhUwSLhUx7PhcAlshlYCEIIhQOIyFgGAmAkCgwC%2BIuswgBiIAoCQqDDMszaVCAkQwH8CDjIBqCmHA0mrOslESOMADUcAAIyJu27DQPAAAGJnmXAWiKA5cBYNQ8AYnAzBWGkHRFAZEBwHpF4GaMfyUdQnC2fZcBOTCplwBZ1kee4BkqUgIliRJzh%2FHsEjad%2B257lUUC1PUjRwM08TQMwfraX0kC4gMjFQRBsG3iB%2FqAMJE%2FrFTpcEIVgyRwHZwjJFcfB8TWgk4blpD5bIkUyXJClKdM2VqISGl%2FGRlx6Z4hnRclLnWXF%2BiTY5zmpa57med5tWeP5gXssEnxhUgEWncZNFXRNCVJbMKVpY9mVzjlonLZ0BXrEVh0nGVFQVVVR61Se9Wbn6ZEtd68BgR1cIxXBpE6XAA2I8NdGeeNk1QNNQA&amp;project=mathlib-release\">link</a> documents my attempts at this topic. Consider it my MWE.<br>\nIn trying to port the recursion scheme library from the Haskell ecosystem to Lean 4, I've repeatedly stumbled in defining a functor fixpoint which is necessary to do so. That's to say, I want to define an operator K such that for a functor F,  (K F) is isomorphic to F (K F). From this stack overflow <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/43083123/how-to-define-a-functor-fixpoint\">post</a>, I initially tried directly translating the Fix type, but Lean's strict positivity checker rejected it. I then tried implementing the Polynomial Functor solution mentioned there, but again, the positivity checker rejected that. Since Lean doesn't implement polarity annotations, I gave up on encoding the Fix type directly. I then tried translating the Mu and Nu fixpoint types from <a href=\"https://hackage.haskell.org/package/data-fix-0.3.3/docs/Data-Fix.html#t:Fix\">here</a>, but since their RHSes is defined as a function whose domain quantifies over arbitrary types, their universe levels are bumped up. Due to this, I cannot seem to express that for an arbitrary functor F, that (Mu F) is isomorphic to F (Mu F) or that (Nu F) is isomorphic to F (Nu F).  </p>\n<p>P.S. I really tried to shorten my MWE, but I don't think I can further compress it without causing others to try my failed attempts.</p>",
        "id": 618117611,
        "sender_full_name": "CalculiLime325",
